
## Extended options order, The Keenest screen without sprites
- Extended options card reordered (named CHEAT_*_ITEM constants): page 1
  SHOW INVENTORY, ZOOM, PLAYERS, UNLIMITED LIVES/AMMO, ALLOW RE-ENTER
  (6 entries); page 2 the rest.
  HIGH SCORES renamed THE KEENEST (hotkey T, loading box "The Keenest").
- The high score screen asked for in the menu or after a battle
  (`high_scores_shown`) no longer replays demo 4: `high_scores_still`
  removes all sprites and only animates the map until a key/button
  (stays forever). The demo loop's high score demo is unchanged.

## Loaded games keep the info plane's scroll blocks
- LoadTheGame only calls RF_NewMap, which keeps just the map-edge blocks;
  the info plane's scroll blocks (25/26) were lost, so a zoomed out view
  of a loaded game showed what lies beyond them (Keen 6 world map, row
  69). `restore_scroll_blocks` re-adds them after loading (not with
  --classic).

## Switch Pro Controller driver (macOS)
- With a Pro Controller (USB, 057e:2009) next to the SNES pads, wrong
  buttons fired constantly: macOS switches it to Nintendo's full report
  mode 0x30, which gilrs parses with the HID descriptor, so the report's
  timer byte showed up as buttons 1-8 (binary counter pattern in
  KEEN_PAD_DEBUG). `src/engine/procon.rs` reads it with hidapi (macOS
  only, non-exclusive: feature macos-shared-device), decodes 0x30
  (positional buttons like SDL, 12-bit sticks), sends the SDL handshake /
  set-mode subcommand if no 0x30 report arrives. `sync_pad` works on
  `PadView` (gilrs pad or Pro state); gilrs' Pro pads and their events are
  skipped while the driver has one open.

## Web browser version (WebAssembly)
- `web/` crate `keen-web` (cdylib, empty except on wasm32), built with
  `wasm-pack build --target web` by web/build.sh into web/dist (static
  page: index.html, main.js, worker.js, audio.js, coi-sw.js, pkg/).
- The game loop blocks like the original, so it runs in a module web
  worker: `Sys` on wasm32 is src/engine/sys_web.rs (same API), sleeping
  with Atomics.wait. Page <-> worker through three SharedArrayBuffers
  (control: key ring, area size, fullscreen, 4 gamepad slots; frame: RGBA
  with a seqlock counter; audio: stereo ring, wrapping i32 frame
  counters). Needs cross-origin isolation: serve.mjs (Node.js/Bun) sends COOP/COEP, else
  coi-sw.js adds them (one reload).
- Sound: the existing Synth (mono) is pulled by Sys (poll/present/wait)
  ~70 ms ahead of the AudioWorklet's read position; while the
  AudioContext is not running (no click yet) it is consumed in real time,
  so sounds still end. rodio is native-only; sound.rs keeps its code with a
  stand-in `rodio` module on wasm (uninhabited Sink).
- Gamepads: navigator.getGamepads() is main-thread only, so the page
  writes buttons (bit mask) and axes per pad; standard mapping is
  translated to the PAD_BUTTONS codes, other pads give RawButton(9<<16|n)
  / RawAxis(n); a hat axis (centered > 1, directions -1..1 in 2/7 steps)
  is detected per pad. The action logic of sync_pad moved to
  `Input::apply_pad` (shared with native).
- Files: src/vfs.rs (std::fs natively; in memory on wasm, filled from the
  page's IndexedDB, writes posted back). Game code (menu saves/config,
  data loading, demo recording, write_png) uses it; SystemTime::now and
  thread::sleep (both panic on wasm32) replaced by vfs::now_since_epoch /
  Sys::sleep_ms. Keys: `input::KEY_CODES` (W3C code names = winit KeyCode
  variant names) replaces the winit match.
- Checked: all 12 original demo traces (K4-6, demos 0-3) identical to the
  previous build; in Chrome (headless, DevTools protocol): title/menu/map,
  keyboard, simulated standard and unmapped (hat) gamepads, START opens
  the menu, audio ring consumed at 44.1 kHz, autosave/config in
  IndexedDB, file picker path, service worker fallback without headers,
  help page fits.
